{#- autoheal (H5.7, docs/target-architecture/host/h5-pi-compose.md). It was an Ansible role's
    compose file until H5.7. The macros that file imported (networks, resources, autokuma labels)
    are written out here, because a service tree has no ansible/templates on its search path.
    The values are the ones the macros rendered for the Pi. This header holds what the role's
    CLAUDE.md said; the DECIDED marker and the environment comments below carry the rest.

    - It watches Docker healthchecks and restarts any container reporting `unhealthy`. Pod
      restarts on the k3s nodes are Kubernetes' own job, so no k8s counterpart exists, and
      daniel-pi is the only host that runs it (Docker was uninstalled on daniel-server on
      2026-08-14).
    - No web UI: nothing to route and nothing for Authelia to gate.
    - AUTOHEAL_CONTAINER_LABEL=all monitors every container that defines a `healthcheck`, with
      no `autoheal=true` label required. A service is therefore self-healing only if its
      healthcheck fails when it is broken. qBittorrent's check probes external egress, not only
      loopback, so an orphaned VPN netns is caught (see the `qbittorrent` role).
    - Every restart posts to Discord, and the payload key is the load-bearing half. The image
      POSTs {"<WEBHOOK_JSON_KEY>": "<message>"} and defaults the key to `text`. Discord reads
      `content` and rejects `text` with a 400. Nothing else sees that failure: autoheal discards
      the response, the container stays healthy and the deploy reads green. ENFORCED by
      hosts/lib/tests/test_pi_compose_autoheal.py. The channel is the shared
      `monitor_discord_webhook_url`. It reaches this file as a `${monitor_discord_webhook_url}`
      reference, which Compose fills from the project's env file (the pi-compose component's
      `outputs:` entry). The Pi's SOPS file must hold that key (`host_secrets.py sync
      daniel-pi`, operator-only).
    - autoheal carries no healthcheck of its own, on purpose. The DECIDED marker below has the
      evidence (#1789). ENFORCED by hosts/lib/tests/test_pi_compose_autoheal.py.
    - AUTOHEAL_START_PERIOD is a startup sleep, not a per-container grace period. Docker
      already suppresses `unhealthy` during a container's own `healthcheck.start_period`. The
      sleep covers the Pi rebooting, when autoheal and everything it watches start together. It
      also blinds autoheal for that long after any deploy that recreates it, which is why it is
      120s.
    - `lifecycle` is its only network: it reaches the write-capable docker-proxy-lifecycle
      there and never joins the broad networks.
    - `depends_on: docker-proxy-lifecycle` replaces the Ansible play's list-order rule. The
      docker-proxy service file in this project declares the sub-proxy. For the seconds between
      compose-adopt removing a container and `up` creating it, no container restarts on an
      unhealthy check.
    - The Watchtower `depends-on` label is not carried over. Watchtower was retired (35c0dc8d)
      and nothing reads the label. -#}
---

services:
  autoheal:
    restart: unless-stopped
    image: willfarrell/autoheal:latest@sha256:a32dfc9bfbd4beb0d467e406d4ee6786765e7544552670189383408ece4f3abe
    tty: true
    container_name: autoheal
    # Read-only rootfs: `docker diff` shows zero writes (busybox sh polling the lifecycle
    # proxy; jq streams in memory) — no tmpfs needed.
    read_only: true
    cap_drop:
      - ALL
    security_opt:
      - no-new-privileges:true
    environment:
      - AUTOHEAL_CONTAINER_LABEL=all
      # Default poll is 5s: each cycle fetches the full container list through the
      # proxy and parses it with jq — constant CPU churn on the Pi (Zero 2 W), where
      # the jq pass alone was visible in top under load (2026-06-11). 30s detection
      # latency for an unhealthy container is fine for this fleet.
      - AUTOHEAL_INTERVAL=30
      # A restart used to leave no trace outside the Pi's own docker logs, so a container
      # flapping was invisible until someone went looking. Reuses the general-purpose
      # `monitor_discord_webhook_url` — the same channel Uptime Kuma and monitor-bridge post
      # to — rather than minting a webhook nobody would remember to rotate.
      - WEBHOOK_URL={{ monitor_discord_webhook_url }}
      # The image POSTs `{"<WEBHOOK_JSON_KEY>": "<message>"}` and defaults the key to `text`
      # (read out of /docker-entrypoint on the live container, 2026-09-10). Discord's webhook
      # API reads `content` and rejects `text` with a 400, so the default would notify nobody
      # while every other signal — container healthy, deploy green — read fine.
      - WEBHOOK_JSON_KEY=content
      # A startup sleep before the FIRST poll, not a per-container grace period: Docker
      # already suppresses `unhealthy` during a container's own healthcheck start_period.
      # What this covers is the Pi rebooting, when autoheal and everything it watches come up
      # together and the slow starters would otherwise be restarted mid-boot. The cost is that
      # a deploy that recreates autoheal also blinds it for this long, which is why it is 120s
      # and not the several minutes a full boot takes.
      - AUTOHEAL_START_PERIOD=120
      - DOCKER_SOCK=tcp://docker-proxy-lifecycle:2375
    healthcheck:
      # DECIDED: autoheal runs with NO healthcheck, so it can never restart ITSELF (#1789).
      # `AUTOHEAL_CONTAINER_LABEL=all` has no self-exclusion — the entrypoint filters on
      # `health=unhealthy` alone — so the image's baked probe (`pgrep -f autoheal`, a sh+pgrep
      # exec through runc) made autoheal its own patient. Under the load a Pi deploy puts on
      # this 512 MB board (load1 8-17, PSI full 20-25%, swap 350 MiB+ in every episode), that
      # exec stalls past Docker's 30s timeout three times running, autoheal reads itself
      # `unhealthy` and issues `docker restart` on its own container, and the `start` half
      # then fails at the OCI runtime ("Timeout waiting for systemd to create scope"). A
      # failed start does not fire `restart: unless-stopped` — that policy covers a process
      # that EXITS — so autoheal sat at Exited/RestartCount 0 until pi-recovery-health's
      # `docker start` arm ran. Observed directly on 2026-09-04 18:47Z ("Container /autoheal
      # found to be unhealthy - Restarting container now", at the window's load1 peak of
      # 17.58); the 2026-08-29 and 2026-09-02/03 stops carry the same load signature and the
      # same failed-start shape. Nothing is lost by dropping the probe: `pgrep` only ever
      # detected a dead process, which `restart: unless-stopped` and the cron's `docker start`
      # both already cover, and a wedged-but-running shell loop passed it anyway. ENFORCED by
      # hosts/lib/tests/test_pi_compose_autoheal.py.
      disable: true
    depends_on:
      - docker-proxy-lifecycle
    networks:
{% for net in container_item.networks %}
      - {{ net }}
{% endfor %}
    labels:
      # Docker-type Kuma monitors are retired fleet-wide (kuma_docker_monitors: false), so the
      # autokuma macro emitted this inert label.
      - "org.homelab.kuma-docker-monitor=retired-slice7-phase-d"
    # Resource caps for blast-radius containment (M1); tune from cAdvisor/Grafana.
    deploy:
      resources:
        limits:
          cpus: '0.25'
          memory: 64M
        reservations:
          cpus: '0.05'
          memory: 16M

networks:
{% for net in container_item.networks %}
  {{ net }}:
    external: true
{% endfor %}
